WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.867 --> 00:00:01.668
[horn honking]

00:00:01.735 --> 00:00:04.003
[siren wailing]

00:00:04.070 --> 00:00:06.773
A city is like a brain.

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:12.112
It develops from a small center
and slowly grows and changes,

00:00:12.178 --> 00:00:16.249
leaving many old parts
still functioning.

00:00:16.316 --> 00:00:19.285
New York can't afford to
suspend its water supply

00:00:19.352 --> 00:00:22.088
or its transportation system
while they're being replaced

00:00:22.155 --> 00:00:24.290
by something more efficient.

00:00:24.357 --> 00:00:29.028
Changes have to
happen piecemeal.

00:00:29.095 --> 00:00:32.699
And that's how it
is for the brain.

00:00:32.766 --> 00:00:36.469
There is no way for evolution
to rip out the ancient interior

00:00:36.536 --> 00:00:39.038
of the brain because
of its imperfections

00:00:39.105 --> 00:00:42.909
and replace it with something
of more modern manufacture.

00:00:42.976 --> 00:00:47.147
The brain and the city both
must function continuously

00:00:47.213 --> 00:00:49.215
during the renovation.

00:00:49.282 --> 00:00:51.684
That's why our limbic
system is surrounded

00:00:51.751 --> 00:00:53.486
by the cerebral cortex.

00:00:53.553 --> 00:00:57.724
The old part is in charge of
too many vital mechanisms for it

00:00:57.791 --> 00:00:59.659
to be replaced altogether.

00:00:59.726 --> 00:01:01.861
So it's sometimes
counterproductive,

00:01:01.928 --> 00:01:05.665
but that's a necessary
consequence of evolution.

00:01:05.732 --> 00:01:09.102
The city is a gift of
the cerebral cortex.

00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:17.444
But the brain's language is
not encoded in the DNA of genes

00:01:17.510 --> 00:01:20.980
because the vocabulary
of life is too small.

00:01:21.047 --> 00:01:25.819
Our brains need a language with
10,000 times as many words.

00:01:25.885 --> 00:01:29.622
The information content of the
human brain expressed in bits

00:01:29.689 --> 00:01:32.258
is probably comparable
to the total number

00:01:32.325 --> 00:01:34.928
of connections
among the neurons,

00:01:34.994 --> 00:01:40.633
about 1,000 trillion bits.

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:43.169
If all the contents
of your brain

00:01:43.236 --> 00:01:46.005
were transcribed into
written language,

00:01:46.072 --> 00:01:47.974
it would amount to
vastly more books

00:01:48.041 --> 00:01:51.544
than are contained in the
largest libraries on Earth.

00:01:51.611 --> 00:01:55.081
The equivalent of more
than 4 billion books

00:01:55.148 --> 00:01:57.350
are inside your head.

00:01:57.417 --> 00:02:02.288
The brain is a very big
place in a very small space.

00:02:06.526 --> 00:02:09.362
It's written in those
neurons pioneered

00:02:09.429 --> 00:02:12.465
by the undersea microbial mats.

00:02:12.532 --> 00:02:15.268
These are tiny
electrochemical switching

00:02:15.335 --> 00:02:18.104
elements, typically a few
hundredths of a millimeter

00:02:18.171 --> 00:02:19.572
across.

00:02:19.639 --> 00:02:22.809
Each of us has 86
billion neurons,

00:02:22.876 --> 00:02:26.446
comparable to the number of
stars in the Milky Way galaxy.

00:02:26.513 --> 00:02:30.850
The neurons and their parts,
axons, dendrites, synapses,

00:02:30.917 --> 00:02:35.255
and the cell bodies themselves
make up a network in the brain.

00:02:35.321 --> 00:02:37.824
Many neurons have
thousands of connections

00:02:37.891 --> 00:02:39.425
with their neighbors.

00:02:39.492 --> 00:02:42.529
Dendrites, those pathways
sent out by neurons

00:02:42.595 --> 00:02:45.598
to connect with other neurons,
extend these nerve cells

00:02:45.665 --> 00:02:49.836
to synapses until they
create a full-blown network

00:02:49.903 --> 00:02:52.138
of consciousness.

00:02:52.205 --> 00:02:56.309
[orchestral music]

00:03:09.856 --> 00:03:13.726
The neurochemistry of the
brain is astonishingly busy,

00:03:13.793 --> 00:03:16.296
the circuitry of a
machine more wonderful

00:03:16.362 --> 00:03:18.998
than any devised by humans.

00:03:19.065 --> 00:03:22.969
Your brain functions are due
to those 100 trillion neural

00:03:23.036 --> 00:03:25.872
connections that make you you.

00:03:25.939 --> 00:03:29.576
Your deepest feelings of love
and awe, those moments when we

00:03:29.642 --> 00:03:32.245
glimpse the grandeur
of nature and all

00:03:32.312 --> 00:03:35.081
the elegant architecture
of consciousness

00:03:35.148 --> 00:03:38.851
are made possible by
those connections.

00:03:38.918 --> 00:03:43.356
This is the essence of
emergence, tiny units of matter

00:03:43.423 --> 00:03:45.525
operating collectively
to become something

00:03:45.592 --> 00:03:48.361
much more than
themselves, to enable

00:03:48.428 --> 00:03:52.131
the cosmos to know itself.

00:03:52.198 --> 00:03:53.833
But there is a
vision of emergence

00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:55.735
that takes it even higher.

00:03:55.802 --> 00:03:58.471
Can we know the universe?

00:03:58.538 --> 00:04:01.007
And will it ever
come to know us?

00:04:01.074 --> 00:04:04.210
[dramatic music]

